; mantine-react-table's newest published release (2.0.0-beta.9) declares a peer
; of @mantine/core@^7.9, and this project is on @mantine/core@8. There is no
; Mantine 8 release of the table, so the conflict cannot be resolved by
; upgrading, and it is not a stale lockfile.
;
; Without this, `npm ci` fails outright with ERESOLVE on any clean checkout —
; which is how it surfaced: the production server deployed fine until the card
; table was added, because before that the conflicting package was not in the
; tree at all.
;
; Scoping it to just this package via `overrides` does not work. npm's
; overrides replace resolved versions; they do not relax a peer range, and the
; ERESOLVE persists. Blanket legacy-peer-deps is the only lever available.
;
; The cost is real: this switches off peer checking for the whole tree, so a
; genuine peer problem in some future dependency will pass silently.
;
; The table does work against Mantine 8 — the peer range is stale, not the
; code. Revisit when mantine-react-table ships a Mantine 8 release, and delete
; this file at that point.
legacy-peer-deps=true
